# How do you choose when the future is uncertain?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why is expected value not enough? What does the St Petersburg paradox teach about utility?"
next: {"title":"Why buy both insurance and lottery tickets?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/85-why-buy-both-insurance-and-lottery.cast"}

## A game with no price
use: data_table
with:
    columns:
      - Heads on toss
      - Chance
      - Prize
      - Adds
    rows:
      - [1, 1/2, $1, 50¢]
      - [2, 1/4, $2, 50¢]
      - [3, 1/8, $4, 50¢]
      - [4, 1/16, $8, 50¢]
      - […, …, …, … forever]
    font_size: 30
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 290
      w: 620
      h: 340
sources:
    - id: hayden2009
      title: The mean, the median, and the St. Petersburg paradox
      authors: Hayden, B. Y. and Platt, M. L.
      year: 2009
      finding: In a real-money version of the St Petersburg game (first prize $1), the median bid of 20 subjects was $1.75.
      url: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3811154
    - id: sep
      title: The St. Petersburg Paradox
      authors: Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy
      year: 2023
      finding: Nicolaus Bernoulli posed the problem in a letter to Montmort in 1713; Daniel Bernoulli's 1738 paper in the St Petersburg Academy's Commentarii gave the paradox its name.
      url: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paradox-stpetersburg/

    icon coin1 size 110 set twemoji x 220 y 500 icon_look picture of coin hidden true
    icon coin2 size 110 set twemoji x 380 y 500 icon_look picture of coin hidden true
    icon coin3 size 110 set twemoji x 540 y 500 icon_look picture of coin hidden true
    text t1 "tails" #8f887c x 220 y 410 font_size 30 hidden true
    text t2 "tails" #8f887c x 380 y 410 font_size 30 hidden true
    text t3 "heads" #2f6b8f x 540 y 410 font_size 30 hidden true
    text prize "→ $4" #2f6b8f x 730 y 500 font_size 64 hidden true
    text rule "$1, doubled by each tails" x 500 y 620 font_size 32 hidden true
    scale guess cites ["hayden2009"] x 120 y 270 width 760 label "Most I'd pay ($)" min 0.5 max 1000 value 1.75 log true hidden true
    text total "Expected value = ∞" #b5482e x 380 y 200 font_size 52 hidden true
    text h1713 "1713\nNicolaus Bernoulli" x 845 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text h1738 "1738\nSt Petersburg" x 845 y 380 font_size 32 hidden true
    icon house size 90 set twemoji x 690 y 200 icon_look picture of house hidden true

    card title "How do you choose when the future is uncertain?"

How do y (@draw coin1@)ou choose when the future is  (@draw coin2@)uncertain? Here is a game (@draw coin3@) to test your answer.

B: A fair coin i (@draw rule@)s tossed until it lands heads. Heads on the first toss pays one dollar, and every ta (@highlight rule effect underline@)ils before it doubles the prize.

So tails, tails, then heads pays fo (@draw prize@)ur dollars.
    draw t1 t2 t3

B: How much would you pay  (@draw guess@)for one go? Be honest: real money.
    erase t1 t2 t3 rule

My gut says about five dollars.
    highlight guess effect pulse

    ask "Set the most you would pay to play once." on guess judge false store pay default "20"
        right: In one 2009 study with real money, people bid a median of a dollar seventy-five.

Mathematicians had a rule for gambles: t (@draw header@)he expected value. Each prize times its chance, all added up.
    erase coin1 coin2 coin3 prize guess

B: Heads at once happens half the time and pays a dollar. That row is w (@highlight row_1 effect underline@)orth fifty cents.
    draw row_1

Heads on the second toss: a quarter of the time, two dollars. Fi (@highlight row_2 effect underline@)fty cents again.
    draw row_2

B: An eighth of the time, four dollars. Every r (@draw row_4@)ow adds fifty cents, and the ro (@draw row_5@)ws never end.
    draw row_3

So the expected value is infinite. By that rule you should pay any price for one go, (@draw house@) your house included.
    draw total

    pause 0.4

B: Nicolaus Berno (@draw h1713@)ulli posed the puzzle in a letter in 1713. It is named after St  (@draw h1738@)Petersburg, where his cousin Daniel published an answer in 1738.

Nobody bids their house. So either everyone is irrational, o (@highlight total effect underline@)r expected value is not the whole story.
    erase h1713 h1738

## Bernoulli's answer
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - Nothing
      - $1,000
      - $1 million
      - You
    values: '{w.vals}'
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      prefix: $
      decimals: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 20
    y_label: Game worth to you ($)
    x_label: Player's wealth
    colors:
      - grey
      - grey
      - orange
      - green
    label_size: 26
    box:
      x: 80
      'y': 170
      w: 600
      h: 450
sources:
    - id: bernoulli1738
      title: Specimen theoriae novae de mensura sortis (Exposition of a new theory on the measurement of risk)
      authors: Bernoulli, D.
      year: 1738
      finding: 'The utility of a gain depends on the wealth of the person who gets it: a gain of a thousand ducats means more to a pauper than to a rich man. Proposes logarithmic utility to resolve the St Petersburg game. English translation: Econometrica 22 (1954) 23–36.'
    - id: calc
      title: 'Own calculation: certainty equivalent of the St Petersburg game (prizes $1, $2, $4 …) under log utility'
      authors: lecture author
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Sure sum c with ln(W + c) = Σ 2^-k ln(W + 2^(k−1)): $2.00 with no other wealth, $5.97 at $1,000, $10.94 at $1 million, $15.92 at $1 billion.'

    ```brython w x 850 y 300 width 280 show code controls ["wealth"] pane controls hidden true
    wealth = Slider(0, 1000000, default=50000, label="Your wealth ($)")
    import math
    def worth(W):
        s = sum(2 ** -k * math.log(W + 2 ** (k - 1)) for k in range(1, 90))
        return round(math.exp(s) - W, 2)
    vals = [worth(0), worth(1000), worth(1000000), worth(wealth)]
    you = round(worth(wealth))
    mine = f"{wealth:,}"
    ```
    text you_read "You: ${w.mine}\nworth ${w.you}" #87a878 x 850 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    portrait bern x 860 y 470 width 200 of "Daniel Bernoulli" cameo true hidden true
    text neq "$1 ≠ $1" #b5482e x 400 y 560 font_size 64 hidden true
    scratch duc work ["+1,000 ducats","pauper: a lot","rich man: a little"] x 400 y 380 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch dbl work ["$1,000 → $2,000","$1 million → $2 million","same step up in U","each extra $ adds less"] x 430 y 430 font_size 32 hidden true
    scratch rep work [{"tex":"\\text{chance: } \\tfrac12,\\ \\tfrac14,\\ \\tfrac18,\\ \\tfrac1{16},\\ \\dots"},{"tex":"\\text{steps of } U\\text{: } 0,\\ 1,\\ 2,\\ 3,\\ \\dots"},{"tex":"\\text{rows: } 0,\\ .25,\\ .25,\\ .19,\\ .13,\\ \\dots \\to \\text{finite}"},"sure sum that feels as good?"] x 480 y 430 font_size 32 hidden true

    card title "Bernoulli's answer"

Dani (@draw bern@)el Bernoulli's answer: a (@draw neq@) dollar is not worth the same to everyone.

A gain o (@draw duc_box duc_line_1@)f a thousand ducats, he wrote, means more (@draw duc_line_2@) to a pauper than to (@draw duc_line_3@) a rich man.

B: (@erase duc bern neq@) His rule: every doubling of your wealth adds the same amount of (@draw dbl_box dbl_line_1@) satisfaction. A thousand to two thousand f (@draw dbl_line_2 dbl_line_3@)eels like a million to two million.

Economists call that satisfaction utility, U. (@draw dbl_line_4@) Each extra dollar adds a little l (@highlight dbl_line_4 effect underline@)ess: diminishing marginal utility.

B: (@erase dbl@) Now replay the game in uti (@draw rep_box rep_line_1@)lity. Each tails doubles the prize, but adds o (@draw rep_line_2@)nly one more step of satisfaction, while its chance halves.

So the rows sh (@draw rep_line_3@)rink, and the sum stops at a finite number. Value the gamble by its expected utility, then as (@draw rep_line_4@)k what sure sum feels just as good.

(@erase rep@) For a  (@draw axes@)player with nothing else, that sure sum is two dolla (@draw bar_1 ends@)rs.

B: Now make (@draw bar_2@) the player a millionaire. Does the game become worth more to her, or less?

Less, surely. What are a few dollars to a millionaire?
    highlight bar_1 bar_2

    ask "Drag the millionaire's bar to what the game is worth to her." on bar_3 store g default "2" voice b
        right: Yes: more, not less.
        wrong: Further up: the game is worth more to a millionaire, not less.

B: More:  (@highlight bar_3@)about eleven dollars. With a thousand  (@highlight bar_2@)dollars, about six.

A prize has to be big next to what you already own before it stops feeling big. The richer you are, the more doublings still count.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

B: Slide your own wealth and watch your bar. Even a billionaire would pay only about sixteen dollars.
    draw w bar_4 you_read parallel true

    explore code w

With one assumption about how money feels, Bernoulli turned infinity into pocket change.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 bar_4

## Or can the bank not pay?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - $1,000
      - $1 million
      - $1 billion
    series:
      - {name: 'Bernoulli: player''s wealth', values: [[5.97, 10.94, 15.92], [5.97, 10.94, 15.92], [5.97, 10.94, 15.92]]}
      - {name: Bank's limit, values: [[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 15.93], [5.98, 10.95, 15.93]]}
    stage: 0
    value_labels: false
    value_format:
      prefix: $
      decimals: 0
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 20
    y_label: Game worth ($)
    x_label: Wealth
    label_size: 28
    box:
      x: 70
      'y': 170
      w: 600
      h: 460
sources:
    - id: calc
      title: 'Own calculation: the St Petersburg game (prizes $1, $2, $4 …) with log utility and with a capped bank'
      authors: lecture author
      year: 2026
      finding: 'Log-utility sure sum at wealth $1,000 / $1 million / $1 billion: $5.97 / $10.94 / $15.92. Expected value when no prize can exceed the bank''s $1,000 / $1 million / $1 billion: $5.98 / $10.95 / $15.93.'
    - id: sep
      title: The St. Petersburg Paradox
      authors: Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy
      year: 2023
      finding: Menger (1934) showed that prizes raised fast enough restore the paradox under any unbounded utility; Buffon (1777) treated probabilities below about 1/10,000 as morally impossible; Buffon and Fontaine raised the limited-resources objection in the 18th century.
      url: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paradox-stpetersburg/

    text menger "Menger, 1934:\nfaster prizes\n→ ∞ again" x 840 y 520 font_size 30 hidden true
    text ceiling "U needs\na ceiling" #b5482e x 840 y 360 font_size 30 hidden true
    text buffon "Buffon, 1777:\n1 in 10,000 ≈ 0" x 840 y 440 font_size 30 hidden true
    text sure1 "Money matters less\nthe more you have" x 840 y 560 font_size 26 hidden true
    text stamp1 "mostly agreed" #2f6b8f x 840 y 480 font_size 34 hidden true
    text sure2 "Utility alone\nsolves the paradox" x 840 y 370 font_size 26 hidden true
    text stamp2 "contested" #b5482e x 840 y 290 font_size 34 hidden true
    icon bank size 110 set twemoji x 840 y 480 icon_look picture of bank hidden true
    text bankq "can it pay?" x 840 y 380 font_size 30 hidden true
    icon next1 size 90 set twemoji x 790 y 470 icon_look picture of umbrella hidden true
    icon next2 size 90 set twemoji x 900 y 470 icon_look picture of ticket hidden true
    text nextq "insurance and\nlottery tickets?" x 845 y 350 font_size 28 hidden true
    text v1 "$6" x 170 y 345 font_size 28 hidden true
    text v2 "$11" x 367 y 442 font_size 28 hidden true
    text v3 "$16" x 567 y 539 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Or can the bank not pay?"

(@draw axes bar_1 bar_2 bar_3 legend v1 v2 v3@) Bernoulli tamed infinity with utility.

B: But here's a simple (@draw bank@)r objection: nobody could pa (@draw bankq@)y those prizes.

Fair. Say the bank holds a  (@highlight bar_3@)billion dollars. After about thirty tails, it simply runs out.

B: Then the rows stop near thirty, and the expected value falls to about sixteen dollars.
    animate stage 1 duration 2

With a thousand dollars in the bank, about six. With a million, about eleven. Bernoulli's numbers, almost to the cent.
    animate stage 2 duration 2.5

B: Both stories cut the game off where the prizes outgrow somebody's wealth: the player's or the bank's. The price alone can't tell them apart.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

    erase bank bankq

And Bernoulli's fix can be broken. In 1934 Karl Menger s (@draw menger@)howed that prizes growing fast enough make even his utility add up to infinity again.

B: To block every such game, util (@draw ceiling@)ity needs a ceiling: past some point, more money adds almost nothing.

(@erase menger ceiling@) Older answers exist too. In 1777 Buffon  (@draw buffon@)argued that a chance below about one in ten thousand is, in practice, zero.

(@erase buffon@) How sure are we? Th (@draw sure1@)at money matters less the more you ha (@draw stamp1@)ve: mostly agreed. (@draw sure2@) That utility alone solves the paradox:  (@draw stamp2@)contested.

So how do you choose when the future is uncertain? Weigh each outcome by its chance, but by what it would mean to you, not by its dollars.
    highlight bar_1 bar_2 bar_3

    quiz "Mia has $1,000 in savings. She can take a sure $1,000, or flip a coin for $2,200 or nothing. What does Bernoulli's rule say?" intro "Two new cases."
        right: The sure $1,000: with her savings it doubles her wealth for certain, and the flip's extra hundred on average can't make up for the risk of getting nothing.
        wrong: The flip's average is higher, but missing out hurts her more than the extra $1,200 would help: here, the sure $1,000.
        * The flip: its expected value is $1,100
        + The sure $1,000 is better for her
        * They are equally good

    quiz "A charity raffles a $50,000 car: tickets cost $10, and each has a 1-in-10,000 chance. What is a ticket's expected value?" voice b
        right: $5: fifty thousand dollars times one in ten thousand. The other $5 is a gift to the charity.
        wrong: Multiply the prize by its chance, $50,000 × 1/10,000: here, $5.
        + $5
        * $10
        * $50,000
        * Nothing: the odds are too long

    erase sure1 stamp1 sure2 stamp2

B: Which leaves a puzzle. If a su (@draw next1 next2 parallel true@)re thing is worth more than a gamble with the same average, why do so many p (@draw nextq@)eople buy both insurance and lottery tickets?

## Where next
